
Block creation includes the current self override for later evaluation and nested closures.
Super sends require a defining class. Start lookup above that class, including
the ordinary missing-method handler and default no-argument initializer when
there is no parent. Unsupported super sends signal instead of returning nil.
Class construction failures signal with the class name and coordinator/error
details, so missing classes cannot make script tests or MCP evaluations succeed.
An unresolved bare identifier in a bound workspace sends a unary message only
when the target supports it or supplies doesNotUnderstand:. Otherwise return nil,
preserving scratch-variable isolation. Explicit sends still signal if unsupported.

Constructor and block spawn requests inherit the explicit generation source bundle so later constructors remain in the originating source version.
